1: # An actual scalar comparison beyond the vertical-edge fan line
2: 
3: Proved as P3K.70 after the root geometric audit and exact certificate, 17 November 2026. This note uses the
4: physical reference convention of K68–69. It retains the full scalar
5: closing and finite-cap maps of K62/K64, and never uses the withdrawn
6: rank-one initial variation of K29. The current target is the explicit
7: OS45 source and the fan with basis ((2,-1),(-1,1)); no universal fan
8: classification is claimed.
9: 
10: ## NV.1. The actual family, parameter and two products
11: 
12: Fix the constructed OS45 surface
13: \[
14:  y^2=x^3-3f(t)x+2g(t),\quad
15:  f=(t^2+1)^2+t,\quad
16:  g=(t^2+1)^3+\tfrac32t(t^2+1)+\tfrac38.
17: \]
18: Its marked section is \(G=(t^2+1,\sqrt3/2)\), its line is
19: \(M=\mathcal O(8G-O)\), and the suspension translates by \(-G\).
20: Retain any one of the four marked simple roots \(\tau\) of
21: \(48t^4+8t^3+96t^2+72t+57\) as the scalar-zero fibre and retain
22: K10's actual ordinary multiplicity-one cap at \(t=0\). The parameter
23: is the primitive \(\lambda=r\), so its scalar winding is one. The
24: root/cap/source construction, properness and sphere topology are the
25: ones already proved in K10/K22; the maps below change its cusp fan
26: and preserve that actual exterior. The forward ordinary closing is
27: \(t/(t-\tau)\), namely \(q^{-1}\) times the bounded unit \(t\) at
28: \(q=t-\tau=0\). No cap is added or removed.
29: 
30: We keep symbols \(J,H,\chi\) temporarily to record exactly the
31: source of each contribution. For this concrete family they are
32: \(J=1,H=8,\chi=1\). On the degree-two parameter cover the full cusp
33: periods are
34: \[
35:  h:(Z,V)\mapsto(\kappa qZ,\alpha V),\qquad
36:  B:(Z,V)\mapsto(gZ,q\eta V),\qquad \eta=\lambda\beta.
37: \tag{NV.1}
38: \]
39: All four bounded units other than \(\lambda\) are the original ones.
40: The forward closing has the form \(q^e u(q)\), with its specified
41: nonzero unit; in the concrete family \(e=-1,u=t\). Reindex by
42: \(B^{-e}\), retaining its source factor, and use K64's actual cut
43: map \(C_\tau=(AZ,DV)\). It satisfies \(C_{\rm end}E=1\) on
44: whole collars, including all derivatives. On the double cover we
45: use exactly \(C^{(2)}_{j+\tau}=C_\tau E^{-j}\), \(j=0,1\).
46: The exterior and cap squares are therefore literal pullbacks of the
47: single family.
48: 
49: The initial two vertex charts are \((a,b,c)\), \((u,v,w)\), with
50: \(q=abc=uvw\). The native fixed star \(C_0\) has charts
51: \[
52:  (z,n_0,n_1)=(b,a,c),\qquad
53:  (z',n'_0,n'_1)=(\kappa w,\alpha v,(\kappa\alpha)^{-1}u).
54: \tag{NV.2}
55: \]
56: Substitution of the full period row gives
57: \(z'=z^{-1}\), \(n'_i=zn_i\), with every unit retained.
58: Its Cox quaternion is
59: \[
60:  q_H=(1+jz)(n_0+j\overline{n_1}).
61: \tag{NV.3}
62: \]
63: This is literally K30.11 in the same order: \(n_0\) has physical
64: weight \(-1\), \(n_1\) weight \(+1\), and physical delta is right
65: multiplication by \(e^{-i\phi}\). Thus the native C0 normal and base
66: markings are the ones used for K69's retained relative pair.
67: 
68: There is one endpoint comparison to retain here. K30 writes its
69: extending origin as a reference-to-current map. Its inverse gives
70: closed coordinates. With linear parameter cutoff and \(g=U^{-1}\),
71: \(\eta=\lambda W^{-1}\), K64's current-to-reference cut map differs
72: from that inverse by the global physical-circle factor
73: \[
74:  \exp\bigl(\pi i eJ(\theta^2+\theta)\bigr)
75: \tag{NV.4}
76: \]
77: on a single \(J\)-winding interval. Both endpoints are one. This is
78: an actual based circle action on the whole family, commuting with all
79: periods and finite caps, so carrying the connection by it preserves
80: the relative return. Replacing the linear cutoff by the protected
81: cutoff changes the bounded source and line factors through their
82: explicit interval logarithms with zero endpoint values. Multiplying
83: these logarithms by a real homotopy parameter gives an actual based
84: homotopy of the star products. The q-dependent normal jets are carried
85: by the normal-rescaling flow of K64. No parameter winding is discarded.
86: 
87: ## NV.2. The fixed-star native-to-ordinary map
88: 
89: For the doubled loop, put \(t=J\) and use exactly K64's common
90: ordinary plumbing product
91: \[
92:  T_i(Q)=H_JQH_{l_i-J},\qquad(l_0,l_1,l_2)=(2J,0,2J).
93: \tag{NV.5}
94: \]
95: At the fixed star, the normal pair in (NV.2) is \((a,c)\), so its
96: literal map to the common product is
97: \[
98:  q_H\longmapsto (U(z)H_JU(z)^{-1})q_HH_J,
99:  \qquad U(z)=\frac{1+jz}{\sqrt{1+|z|^2}}.
100: \tag{NV.6}
101: \]
102: This is obtained by substituting \(Q=n_0+j\overline{n_1}\) into
103: (NV.3), with no interchange or inversion of the normal pair.
104: At the opposite vertex the factors are precisely the full unit
105: factors \(\eta/\alpha\) and \(\kappa\alpha\) from K64. The
106: normal homotopy therefore matches both protected collars of (NV.2).
107: 
108: The left factor in (NV.6) has degree \(2J\) on the oriented native
109: Hopf base times parameter, by the same direct quaternion integral as
110: SV.11; the right factor depends only on radius and parameter and has
111: zero third degree. The inverse-cylinder extension has endpoint
112: \(L^{-2J}\). It follows that the ordinary relative pair is the
113: native pair minus \((2J,0)\). This is the identical arrow to the
114: already proved nonfixed C1/C2 comparison, now verified in the actual
115: fixed-star coordinates (NV.2)–(NV.3).
116: 
117: The retained K69 native pair on the single family is
118: \((6\chi,3H-6\chi)\). Its literal double pullback is
119: \((12\chi,6H-12\chi)\), hence the common-product C0 pair is
120: \[
121:  (m_0^{\rm ord},n_0^{\rm ord})
122:        =(12\chi-2J,6H-12\chi).
123: \tag{NV.7}
124: \]
125: The actual product-frame derivative map K31.7, retained independently
126: of its false initial-data claim, therefore gives
127: \[
128:  V_{00}=2J-6H,\qquad \delta_0=2J+6H-24\chi.
129: \tag{NV.8}
130: \]
131: The physical double return is \(2(H/2-\chi)\), by the actual
132: parameter pullback and K68, not by a division of a sphere class.
133: 
134: ## NV.3. Complete initial ordinary data from the actual prisms
135: 
136: The full-unit trace maps of K64 have degrees
137: \[
138:  I=(-2J,2J,-2J),\qquad W=(2J,0,0).
139: \tag{NV.9}
140: \]
141: Use the literal generic endpoint paths and rank-one endpoint caps of
142: K51/K52. Their vertex maps are (SV.5), commute with the physical
143: circle and are parameter independent after transport. Thus the same
144: oriented chains give
145: \[
146:  \operatorname{Var}(D_1)=2J(m_0+m_1),\qquad
147:  \operatorname{Var}(D_2)=2J(-m_0+m_2),\qquad
148:  \delta_1=-2J,\quad\delta_2=2J.
149: \tag{NV.10}
150: \]
151: Here \(\Delta x_1=D_1\), \(\Delta x_2=-D_2\), and the boundary
152: identity is \(\operatorname{Var}(\Delta x)=[K\partial x]\).
153: Together with symmetry and (NV.8), the actual data are
154: \[
155:  V_0=\begin{pmatrix}
156:  2J-6H&2J&-2J\\2J&2J&0\\-2J&0&2J
157:  \end{pmatrix},\qquad
158:  \delta_0^{\rm vec}=
159:  \begin{pmatrix}2J+6H-24\chi\\-2J\\2J\end{pmatrix}.
160: \tag{NV.11}
161: \]
162: These replace the withdrawn scalar rank-one seed. Their derivation
163: uses the actual separate native products and the ordinary double
164: product; it makes no simultaneous equivariance assertion.
165: 
166: ## NV.4. Two literal Cox changes and target products
167: 
168: The first flip is C0, with exact new basis and characters
169: \[
170:  P_1=\begin{pmatrix}1&-1\\0&1\end{pmatrix},\qquad
171:  (Z',V')=(ZV,V).
172: \tag{NV.12}
173: \]
174: The native other-ruling map is
175: \((z,n_0,n_1)\mapsto(n_0/n_1,zn_1,n_1)\); the rank-one matrix
176: \(\bigl(\begin{smallmatrix}n_0&n_1\\zn_0&zn_1\end{smallmatrix}\bigr)\)
177: gives both charts. It preserves \(q=zn_0n_1\) exactly. The whole
178: unit matrix transforms by the actual monomial map
179: \(A'_{rs}=\prod A_{hj}^{(P_1^{-1})_{rh}(P_1)_{js}}\).
180: The cut map C transforms by the same characters and has the full Cox
181: lift. Thus the outside source, finite cap and closing remain the
182: ones of NV.1.
183: 
184: Construct the new ordinary product from these full transformed units,
185: using the same based logarithms and H maps. On this fan its doubled
186: winding matrix, tangent/normal weights and first normal exponents are
187: \[
188:  B_1=\begin{pmatrix}0&2J\\0&2J\end{pmatrix},\quad
189:  w=(1,1,-2),\quad d=(-3,-3,0),\quad l=(2J,-2J,2J).
190: \tag{NV.13}
191: \]
192: The trace maps, including their full unit homotopies, have
193: \[
194:  I=(-2J,10J,-2J),\qquad W=(4J,-8J,-2J).
195: \tag{NV.14}
196: \]
197: The selected new star is C2. The unrestricted four-weight Cox
198: common-link and the constant complex reset are precisely K54/CF.5.
199: On each side the native-to-ordinary map adds the same pure-left
200: \(-2J\) to the native return. It cancels in the selected-coordinate
201: comparison. Consequently, if a,b are the old selected ordinary
202: \(V_{00},\delta_0\), the new selected entries are
203: \(V'_{22}=a\), \(\delta'_2=2a-b\), and the physical central
204: increment is the old native right integer. This is a direct comparison
205: of the two constructed products on the selected star; no common
206: exterior matrix-reset premise is used.
207: 
208: All target weights are nonzero. Its actual prism equations and
209: symmetry then determine the target uniquely from those two selected
210: entries: with \(r_i=1/w_i\),
211: \[
212:  V'=\operatorname{diag}(I_i/w_i)+c\,rr^t,\quad
213:  \delta'_i=(W_i+e_*)/w_i,\quad
214:  c=w_2^2a-w_2I_2,\quad e_*=w_2(2a-b)-W_2.
215: \tag{NV.15}
216: \]
217: This is linear algebra on actual integral cycle identities. Substitution
218: gives
219: \[
220:  V'=\begin{pmatrix}
221:  2J-24H&4J-24H&-2J+12H\\
222:  4J-24H&14J-24H&-2J+12H\\
223:  -2J+12H&-2J+12H&2J-6H
224:  \end{pmatrix},\quad
225:  \delta'=\begin{pmatrix}
226:  2J+36H-48\chi\\-10J+36H-48\chi\\2J-18H+24\chi
227:  \end{pmatrix}.
228: \tag{NV.16}
229: \]
230: The new C1 ordinary and native right integer is therefore
231: \[
232:  n_1^{(2)}=30H-24\chi-12J.
233: \tag{NV.17}
234: \]
235: Its native product is the explicit periodic holomorphic star product
